use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/FormParticipation import FormParticipation

export ui-component Switch:
    description:
        A switch turns a setting on or off, like a light switch. Flipping it
        takes effect straight away, with no Save or OK step.

    behaviors:
        - @{FormParticipation}

    states:
        On or off, with nothing in between. Flipping it switches the state and
        tells the component using it. That component can also set the state at
        any time, such as switching it back if the change couldn't be applied.

    name:
        The name says what the setting is, such as "Wi-Fi", and stays the same
        whether it's on or off. Whether it's on is conveyed separately.

    activation:
        The user flips it the usual way for the platform: clicking, tapping, or
        using the keyboard. Clicking its label also flips it. Which keys do
        this, and whether the knob can be dragged, is up to the chosen library.

    forms:
        A switch inside a form still takes effect straight away, and its state
        is also sent with the form, the way FormParticipation describes. That
        lets a settings page work both ways.

    disabled:
        A disabled switch can't be flipped, and it's clear to the user that it's
        unavailable. It keeps showing whether it's on or off. A read-only
        switch can be focused and read out, but not flipped.

    nonGoals:
        - No partly-on state. That's a Checkbox
        - Not for choices that only take effect when a form is saved. That's a
            Checkbox
